{"name":"Garden of Paradise","hex":"#8a8d66","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"garden","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An outdoor area containing one or more types of plants, usually plants grown for food or ornamental purposes.","Such an ornamental place to which the public have access."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To grow plants in a garden; to create or maintain a garden.","Of a batsman, to inspect and tap the pitch lightly with the bat so as to smooth out small rough patches and irregularities."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/garden"},{"term":"paradise","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The place where sanctified souls are believed to live after death.","A very pleasant place, such as a place full of lush vegetation."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Paradise (“Heaven”).","Alternative letter-case form of Paradise (“the Garden of Eden”)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/paradise"}],"entries":[{"title":"Garden of Eden","blocks":[{"text":"The Garden of Eden, also called Garden of God or the (Terrestrial) Paradise, is the biblical paradise described in Genesis 2–3 and Ezekiel 28 and 31."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Jan_Brueghel_de_Oude_en_Peter_Paul_Rubens_-_Het_aards_paradijs_met_de_zondeval_van_Adam_en_Eva.jpg/330px-Jan_Brueghel_de_Oude_en_Peter_Paul_Rubens_-_Het_aards_paradijs_met_de_zondeval_van_Adam_en_Eva.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garden_of_Eden","wikidata":"Q19014"},{"title":"Garden","blocks":[{"text":"A garden is a planned space, usually outdoors, set aside for the cultivation, display, and enjoyment of plants and other forms of nature. The single feature identifying even the wildest wild garden is control. The garden can incorporate both natural and artificial materials."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Brooklyn_Botanic_Garden_New_York_May_2015_010.jpg/330px-Brooklyn_Botanic_Garden_New_York_May_2015_010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garden","wikidata":"Q1107656"},{"title":"Paradise","blocks":[{"text":"In religion and folklore, paradise is a place of everlasting happiness, delight, and bliss. Paradisiacal notions are often laden with pastoral imagery, and may be cosmogonical, eschatological, or both, often contrasted with the miseries of human civilization: in paradise there is only peace, prosperity, and happiness. Paradise is a place of contentment, a land of luxury and fulfillment containing ever-lasting bliss and delight. Paradise is often described as a \"higher place\", the holiest place, in contrast to this world, or underworlds such as hell."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Angelico%2C_giudizio_universale_01.jpg/330px-Angelico%2C_giudizio_universale_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paradise","wikidata":"Q3363340"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/James_Jacques_Joseph_Tissot_-_Adam_and_Eve_Driven_From_Paradise_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/500px-James_Jacques_Joseph_Tissot_-_Adam_and_Eve_Driven_From_Paradise_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/James_Jacques_Joseph_Tissot_-_Adam_and_Eve_Driven_From_Paradise_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/1280px-James_Jacques_Joseph_Tissot_-_Adam_and_Eve_Driven_From_Paradise_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/James_Jacques_Joseph_Tissot_-_Adam_and_Eve_Driven_From_Paradise_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/500px-James_Jacques_Joseph_Tis